body:
I've made several experimental changes to the raytracing API to address some potential pitfalls I'm concerned about.

## Acceleration structure instances

I added an acceleration structure instance struct that maps directly to the instance descriptors used in D3D12/Vulkan/Metal. The only missing part is the hit group index, which will need to be addressed later. Previously, only the BLAS and the transform could be specified.

```cpp
enum AccelerationStructureInstanceFlagBits {
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_INSTANCE_TRIANGLE_FACING_CULL_DISABLE_BIT = (1 << 0),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_INSTANCE_TRIANGLE_FLIP_FACING_BIT = (1 << 1),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_INSTANCE_FORCE_OPAQUE_BIT = (1 << 2),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_INSTANCE_FORCE_NO_OPAQUE_BIT = (1 << 3),
};

struct AccelerationStructureInstance {
	Transform3D transform;
	uint32_t id = 0;
	uint8_t mask = 0xFF;
	BitField<AccelerationStructureInstanceFlagBits> flags = {};
	RID blas;
};
```

## Acceleration structure flags

These map directly to the flags available in D3D12/Vulkan/Metal. Previously, the Vulkan backend always forced fast trace bit, which is not ideal for animated models.

```cpp
enum AccelerationStructureFlagBits {
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_ALLOW_UPDATE_BIT = (1 << 0),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_ALLOW_COMPACTION_BIT = (1 << 1),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_PREFER_FAST_TRACE_BIT = (1 << 2),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_PREFER_FAST_BUILD_BIT = (1 << 3),
	ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_LOW_MEMORY_BIT = (1 << 4),
};
```

## TLAS instances buffer

I removed this API. Apparently, the idea behind exposing it was to allow the GPU to populate the instance buffer directly: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/99119#discussion_r1987836073

I don't really see a way for this to work correctly:
- The instance descriptor layout differs on Metal.
- There is no way to track which BLASes are referenced inside the buffer.

While I will not be implementing it in this PR, I have a possible alternative design:

```cpp
// The GPU side struct.
struct AccelerationStructureIndirectInstance {
	Transform3D transform;
	uint32_t id = 0;
	uint8_t mask = 0xFF;
    uint32_t reserved = 0; // Reserved for future hit group index.
	BitField<AccelerationStructureInstanceFlagBits> flags = {};
	uint32_t blas_index = 0;
};

struct AccelerationStructureIndirectInstanceBuffer {
    RID buffer;
    uint32_t offset = 0;
    uint32_t count = 0;
    Span<RID> blases;
};

Error tlas_build(RID p_tlas, Span<AccelerationStructureInstance> p_instances, Span<AccelerationStructureIndirectInstanceBuffer> p_indirect_instance_buffers);
```

Users can provide buffers following the struct layout defined by Godot. Internally, the graphics backend would run a compute shader to translate this layout into the backend specific instance format.

Since BLAS references would also index into the CPU provided BLAS list, the engine would be able to track them correctly.

The instance buffer also had a race condition where it could be written to while the previous frame was using it to build the TLAS. I changed it to create multiple internal instance buffers unique to every frame.

## Draw tracker management

The previous implementation did not work correctly.

I replaced it with an implementation that creates weak links between TLASes and BLASes. When a BLAS is rebuilt or freed, any TLAS that referenced it is invalidated and must be rebuilt. In practice, this should be fine since TLASes rarely remain static between frames and typically need rebuilding anyway.

The weak link preserves the TLAS object's internal memory and avoids destroying uniform sets that depend on it. If a hard dependency were added through `_add_dependency`, any BLAS modification would destroy the TLAS object entirely, and uniform sets that depend on it.

To detect invalid states, I added error checks that trigger when:
- BLASes are provided in TLAS build despite requiring rebuilding.
- TLASes are bound through uniform sets despite requiring rebuilding.

BLASes should also ideally be invalidated if any write operations occur on the buffers they depend on, but this is not implemented yet.

## Acceleration structure build input usage

I removed this. It was used for both BLASes and vertex/index buffers, which is incorrect. Buffer inputs in BLAS need `VK_ACCESS_SHADER_READ_BIT`, which maps to `RESOURCE_USAGE_STORAGE_BUFFER_READ`.

